**Problem.** Find all $f, g, h: \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R}^+$ such that
$$f(g(h(x)) + y) + h(z + f(y)) = g(y) \,+\, h(y + f(z)) \,+\, x \qquad \forall\, x,y,z>0. \tag{P}$$

**Answer.** $\;f(x)=x,\; h(x)=x,\; g(x)=x+c$ for an arbitrary constant $c\ge 0$.

---

### Proof

Denote the assertion of (P) at $(x,y,z)$ by $P(x,y,z)$. Rearranging,
$$f(g(h(x))+y)-x \;=\; g(y)+h(y+f(z))-h(z+f(y)). \tag{1}$$
The left side depends only on $(x,y)$ and the right only on $(y,z)$; hence both equal some function $\phi(y)$. Set $A(x):=g(h(x))$. Then
$$f(A(x)+y)=x+\phi(y), \qquad g(y)+h(y+f(z))-h(z+f(y))=\phi(y). \tag{2}$$

**Step 1. $A$ is injective, hence $h$ is injective.**
If $A(x_1)=A(x_2)$, then by (2) $x_1+\phi(y)=x_2+\phi(y)$, so $x_1=x_2$. Thus $A$ is injective. Consequently $h$ is injective: if $h(a)=h(b)$, then $g(h(a))=g(h(b))$, i.e. $A(a)=A(b)$, so $a=b$.

**Step 2. $h(y+f(z))=h(z+f(y))$ forces $f(x)=x+c$.**
From the second equation in (2), swapping $y$ and $z$ gives
$$g(z)+h(z+f(y))-h(y+f(z))=\phi(z).$$
Adding this to the original second equation yields $\phi(y)+\phi(z)=g(y)+g(z)$, so $\phi(y)=g(y)$ for all $y$. Hence
$$h(y+f(z))=h(z+f(y)) \qquad \forall\, y,z>0. \tag{3}$$
Since $h$ is injective (Step 1), (3) gives $y+f(z)=z+f(y)$, i.e.
$$y-f(y)=z-f(z) \qquad \forall\, y,z>0.$$
So $y-f(y)$ is a constant $-c$; thus
$$f(y)=y+c \qquad (y>0). \tag{4}$$
The requirement $f:\mathbb{R}^+\to\mathbb{R}^+$ gives $y+c>0$ for all $y>0$, hence $c\ge 0$.

**Step 3. Determine $g$ and $h$.**
Substituting (4) into $f(A(x)+y)=x+g(y)$ (from (2), with $\phi=g$):
$$A(x)+y+c = x+g(y) \;\Longrightarrow\; A(x)=x+g(y)-y-c.$$
The left side is independent of $y$, so $g(y)-y$ is a constant $d$:
$$g(y)=y+d, \qquad d\ge 0 \;\;(\text{from } g:\mathbb{R}^+\to\mathbb{R}^+). \tag{5}$$
Then $A(x)=x+d-c$, i.e. $g(h(x))=x+d-c$. Using $g(t)=t+d$:
$$h(x)+d = x+d-c \;\Longrightarrow\; h(x)=x-c. \tag{6}$$
The requirement $h:\mathbb{R}^+\to\mathbb{R}^+$ gives $x-c>0$ for all $x>0$, hence $c\le 0$.

Combining $c\ge 0$ (Step 2) and $c\le 0$ (Step 3): $\boxed{c=0}$.

Therefore
$$f(x)=x, \qquad h(x)=x, \qquad g(x)=x+d \;\;(d\ge 0).$$

**Step 4. Verification.** With $f(x)=x,\; g(x)=x+d,\; h(x)=x$:
$$\text{LHS}=f(g(h(x))+y)+h(z+f(y))=(x+d+y)+(z+y)=x+d+2y+z,$$
$$\text{RHS}=g(y)+h(y+f(z))+x=(y+d)+(y+z)+x=x+d+2y+z.$$
LHS$=$RHS for all $x,y,z>0$. ✓

---

All solutions are $f(x)=x,\; h(x)=x,\; g(x)=x+c$ with $c\ge 0$ arbitrary.

